---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/advanced/clinical-trials-and-evidence">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Medicine's central epistemic problem: patients get better for reasons that have nothing to do
    with the treatment. Diseases resolve spontaneously; symptoms fluctuate and regression to the mean
    guarantees that people seek care at their worst; the act of being treated (attention, ritual,
    expectation) has real physiological effects. Before randomisation, medicine was a graveyard of
    therapies that "obviously worked" — bloodletting among them — because experience without control
    cannot separate treatment effect from natural history. The randomised controlled trial is the
    design that solves this, and everything in this chapter is either how it works, how it scales
    into a drug-development pipeline, or how to read one honestly.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four phases and the attrition between them" widget="equationStep" config={trialsCfg} />

  <h2>Randomisation: why it is the load-bearing wall</h2>
  <p>
    The problem any comparison faces is confounding — patients who receive the new treatment differ
    systematically from those who do not (they are sicker, or richer, or treated at better centres),
    and those differences, not the drug, may drive the outcome. Randomisation severs the link: if
    allocation is truly by chance, then <em>in expectation</em> the arms are identical on every
    variable — measured or not, known or not. That is the magic: no other design (matching,
    adjustment, propensity scores) balances the unknown confounders, because you cannot adjust for
    what you did not measure. Blinding then protects the comparison at its other choke points:
    patients report differently when they know (expectation effects), clinicians treat and measure
    differently (performance bias), and outcome assessors code events differently (detection bias).
    The full architecture is allocation concealment (the randomisation sequence hidden from enrolment
    decisions), double-blinding, and intention-to-treat analysis — analyse everyone as randomised,
    including dropouts and protocol violators, because the moment you clean up the groups you
    re-import the confounding randomisation removed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example of the failure mode.</strong> Suppose a surgical innovation is adopted by
    enthusiastic pioneers, while control patients receive conventional care during a period when a
    hospital renovation disrupted wards. Non-randomised comparison: new surgery, 80% survival;
    conventional, 65%. Randomise (REPAIR-like trials of this era) and the gap collapses or reverses
    — the pioneers selected healthier candidates, and the renovation period selected sicker ones.
    History is rich with these reversals: hormone replacement therapy looked cardioprotective in
    observational cohorts (women who took it were healthier in every measurable way) and showed harm
    in the Women's Health Initiative randomised trial. The observational-versus-randomised gap is
    the recurring object lesson of evidence-based medicine.
  </p>

  <h2>The pipeline: phases and their arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    Drug development is a funnel with brutal attrition: from roughly 5,000 screened compounds, one
    reaches approval; the process takes 10–15 years and costs on the order of a billion dollars
    (estimates range 0.7–2.6 billion including failures). Phase I establishes human safety and dose
    (healthy volunteers, escalation rules, pharmacokinetics); Phase II asks for an efficacy signal in
    patients and kills most candidates — the attrition cliff; Phase III is the confirmatory gamble,
    large, multi-site, powered for the approval decision; Phase IV is the market as laboratory. Each
    phase's design inherits the previous phase's uncertainty: an under-dosed Phase III kills a good
    drug, an over-dosed one poisons it — dose selection is where trials most often fail.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Power, computed.</strong> How big must Phase III be? The rough rule for a dichotomous
    endpoint: n per arm ≈ 16 × p(1−p) ÷ δ², where p is the control-event rate and δ the absolute
    improvement worth detecting, with 80% power at p = 0.05. Control mortality 20%, hoped reduction
    to 15% (δ = 0.05): n ≈ 16 × 0.2 × 0.8 ÷ 0.0025 = 1,024 per arm — over 2,000 patients, years of
    recruitment. Try to detect δ = 0.02 and the requirement grows to ~6,400 per arm. This arithmetic
    is why trials are powered for the <em>sponsor's</em> question, and why "no significant
    difference" in a 40-patient study means almost nothing: absence of evidence is not evidence of
    absence, and the confidence interval is the honest way to say what the study could and could not
    exclude.
  </p>

  <h2>Endpoints: what you measure is what you know</h2>
  <p>
    Clinical endpoints (death, stroke, fracture) are gold but slow and expensive; surrogate endpoints
    (a cholesterol value, tumour shrinkage, a biomarker) are fast and measurable. The recurring
    disaster of therapeutics is surrogates that decoupled: anti-arrhythmics suppressed ectopic beats
    (the surrogate) and increased mortality (the outcome) — the CAST trial's finding killed a
    standard practice that had been confidently treating for years. Surrogates are acceptable only
    when validated — shown repeatedly to track the clinical endpoint in this drug class, this
    population. Modern accelerated-approval pathways deliberately trade on this: conditional approval
    on surrogate response rates, with confirmatory trials (increasingly, with withdrawal teeth)
    required afterwards. Know which endpoint a headline is using before believing it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "statistically significant" means clinically important. A huge
    trial can find a statistically robust effect of trivial size (relative risk reduction 3% on a
    0.4% baseline event rate); a small trial can find an enormous effect that fails p = 0.05. The
    professional reading reports <strong>absolute risk reduction</strong> and the
    <strong>number needed to treat</strong>: a statin cutting 10-year risk from 20% to 15% (ARR 5%)
    gives NNT = 20; the same relative benefit in a 2%-risk patient gives ARR 0.5% and NNT = 200.
    Same drug, same trial, different patient — the entire art of individualised prescribing is this
    recomputation.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading a paper critically</h2>
  <p>
    The checklist, in the order it usually breaks. <strong>Was it registered, and were the endpoints
    pre-specified?</strong> Outcome switching — reporting the endpoint that worked after seeing the
    data — was common enough that registries (clinicaltrials.gov, mandated for publication in major
    journals) exist to catch it; compare registered primary outcome to reported one. <strong>Is the
    comparison fair?</strong> Active controls beat placebos for ethics, but an under-dosed comparator
    manufactures superiority. <strong>Intention-to-treat?</strong> Per-protocol analyses re-import
    selection bias. <strong>Are the subgroups pre-specified?</strong> Twenty-four reported subgroups
    guarantee one "significant" finding at p = 0.05 by chance alone. <strong>Are harms reported with
    the same energy as benefits?</strong> Trials systematically under-report adverse events. The
    CONSORT checklist exists because each of these failures recurred often enough to need a name.
    Then the highest-level move: place the result in the evidence hierarchy — systematic reviews and
    meta-analyses of pre-registered randomised trials sit at the summit, with heterogeneity (I²)
    telling you whether studies agree enough to pool, and funnel-plot asymmetry or registry-comparison
    studies exposing <strong>publication bias</strong> — the systematic disappearance of negative
    trials, quantified for antidepressants (turnaround study: FDA registry data nearly halved the
    published effect size).
  </p>

  <h2>The regulatory layer: approval and after</h2>
  <p>
    Trials exist inside a regulatory architecture. Regulators (FDA, EMA, MHRA and
    counterparts) define evidentiary standards: Phase III typically requires adequate
    well-controlled evidence, pre-specified endpoints, and benefit that justifies risk for
    the <em>indicated population</em> — approvals are narrow for a reason, and off-label use
    is the medical system's risk transfer. Accelerated pathways (conditional approval,
    breakthrough designation, priority review) trade surrogate endpoints for speed with
    confirmatory obligations; the accelerated-approval withdrawal mechanism — finally
    exercised meaningfully in the 2020s (an Alzheimer's drug's marketing withdrawn, several
    oncology indications retracted after failed confirmatory trials) — is the system's
    self-correction being tested in public. <strong>Pharmacovigilance</strong> is the
    permanent phase: spontaneous-report systems (where under-reporting and reporting bias
    live), mandated registries (pregnancy exposure, long-term safety), periodic safety
    updates, and signal-detection algorithms scanning databases for patterns. The regulatory
    story is also political economy: user fees fund reviewers, trial obligations shape where
    trials run (increasingly global, with ethics questions about populations that bear risk
    without assured access), and patent timelines (the 20-year clock runs from filing, so
    effective market exclusivity is much shorter) shape which trials get run at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Vaccines: the pipeline's fastest modern case</h2>
  <p>
    The COVID-19 vaccine programme compressed the standard sequence without skipping its
    logic: Phase I safety (mRNA platforms' first human doses in 2020), Phase II
    immunogenicity and dose selection, and Phase III efficacy trials of unprecedented size
    (30,000–45,000 participants, event-driven analysis) read out in under a year — possible
    because funding removed the usual serial pauses and the epidemic's scale supplied
    endpoints quickly, not because the evidentiary bar moved. The trial results (efficacy
    around 90% against symptomatic disease in the initial window) and the subsequent
    real-world evidence (strong persistent protection against severe disease, waning
    effectiveness against infection as variants emerged) are the best modern teaching case
    for this entire chapter: endpoints, surrogates, generalisability and pharmacovigilance,
    all at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Designs beyond the classic RCT.</strong> The RECOVERY platform trial (COVID era) showed
    what adaptive, multi-arm infrastructure can do: one protocol, many treatment arms entering and
    leaving as evidence accumulates, tens of thousands of patients, and life-saving answers
    (dexamethasone) in months instead of years. Adaptive designs, Bayesian interim analyses and
    master-protocols are now mainstream; equivalence and non-inferiority designs (asking "is the new
    drug not-worse," for when a biosimilar cannot ethically be tested against placebo) require
    reading the margin, not the p-value. <strong>Fraud and replication.</strong> The Surgisphere
    scandal (2020) — retracted Lancet and NEJM papers built on a database that could not exist —
    forced journal-level reform and is the modern case study in why data sharing matters; retired
    classics like Wakefield's autism fraud show the social cost of evidence failure.
    <strong>The generalisability problem.</strong> Trials exclude exactly the patients clinicians
    treat: the elderly, multi-morbid, pregnant, and polypharmacy-adjacent — median trial participants
    remain younger, whiter and male-skewed relative to disease populations, and regulators increasingly
    demand diversity action plans. Real-world evidence (claims data, registries) can extend
    conclusions but re-opens the confounding door — its honest use is hypothesis plus calibration
    against trials. <strong>Open questions:</strong> how to run trials during epidemics at the speed
    pathogens move; whether peer review adds measurable quality; and whether AI-drafted protocols and
    synthetic control arms (borrowing historical patient data as comparators) will shrink trials
    faster than they import bias. Evidence-based medicine, it turns out, needs evidence about itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Health economics completes the decision architecture. The randomised trial answers "does
    it work?"; the cost-effectiveness analysis answers "is it worth it?" — quality-adjusted
    life-years (QALYs) gained per pound, with thresholds (historically around £20,000–30,000
    per QALY at NICE) rationing implicitly. The arithmetic is consequential and contested:
    QALYs weight years by quality, which can disadvantage disabled populations (a documented
    equity critique), and the thresholds are administrative conventions, not laws of nature.
    The fuller frame — cost per life saved, value-of-statistical-life estimates, discounting
    of future health — makes explicit what every health system decides implicitly. And the
    access question closes the loop back to trials: pricing negotiations use the trial
    evidence, so trial design (which comparator, which population, which endpoint) ultimately
    shapes who gets the drug. The advanced reading of any clinical paper now includes its
    economic evaluation — because approval, reimbursement and access are one pipeline with
    different gates. The lesson generalises beyond medicine: any evidence-based policy
    eventually meets a budget, and the meeting is where the evidence's real-world meaning is
    set.
  </p>

